149
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or less.

150
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
If you're taking your scouts to summer camp this summer

151
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and you're going to spend a week

152
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
out there away from home,

153
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
it's likely you will be dealing

154
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
with one or more homesick scouts

155
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Now. I come from an era when homesickness

156
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
was regarded as a weakness

157
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
or something to be ashamed of,

158
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
But I want to make sure that we all understand

159
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
that preventing and treating homesickness

160
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
needs to be approached like administering first aid

161
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
for any other illness or injury.

162
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Homesickness is not an imaginary thing.

163
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
It's not an indication of weakness

164
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
or a lack of character.

165
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
It's not a symptom of bad parenting

166
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
or bad leadership

167
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
And, thankfully, in most cases

168
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
it's pretty easy to prevent

169
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
and pretty easy to treat.

170
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Scout leaders and parents of scouts

171
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
should consider following

172
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
a homesickness prevention plan

173
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
well before scouts are headed to camp.

174
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
There are things that scout leaders need to do

175
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
and things that parents need to do.

176
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,000
For scout leaders,

177
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
I would suggest making a discussion

178
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
of summer camp a regular feature

179
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
of unit meetings leading up to your stay at camp.

180
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Talk about the program and the accommodations,

181
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
the games, the events and the daily routine

182
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
to help scouts familiarize themselves

183
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,000
with the experience,

184
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Share pictures and patches

185
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and awards from past camps.

186
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Just talk it up real good,

187
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
and this is something that is best done

188
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
by scouts who've been to camp before right.

189
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
If it's possible,

190
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
you may even be able to arrange to have a camp staff member

191
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,000
visit a unit meeting

192
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and talk to scouts about camp.

193
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
If you can arrange this,

194
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
maybe you can also get them to welcome your scouts

195
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
when they arrive at camp.

196
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
We need to very consciously work with parents

197
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
to establish a contact plan

198
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
while we're at camp.

199
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Most experts agree that real-time contact

200
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
like phone calls or texting and email

201
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
can complicate rather than alleviate homesickness

202
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
if the stay away from home

203
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
is less than several weeks long.

204
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Letter writing, it turns out, is a better alternative.

205
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,000
If you're going to sit down

206
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and write a letter home,

207
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
it's going to require a scout to reflect

208
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
on what he's doing,

209
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
and these reflections often aid

210
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
in alleviating the anxiety

211
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
that causes homesick.

212
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
With a parent's role,

213
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
they need to make the stay at camp visible at home

214
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
well ahead of the time a scout's going to leave

215
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
to go to camp.

216
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Mark it on the calendar

217
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,000
or post it on the fridge

218
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and show your scout how long they will be away.

219
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
that can lead to a discussion of any anxiety

220
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,000
they may have about it.

221
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
When the scout's ready to head off for camp,

222
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
provide your scout with some pre-stamped,

223
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
pre-addressed envelopes and paper

224
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
or postcards or both,

225
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
and you might even have them practice writing

226
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and sending a letter at home.

227
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Sending a letter through the US mail

228
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
has become less and less common

229
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and I don't think we should assume

230
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
that all of our scouts know how to do that.

231
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Parents can also encourage scouts

232
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
to include a few transitional objects

233
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
or mementos while they're packing for camp,

234
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
and these could be, you know,

235
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,000
a family picture,

236
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
or maybe there's a stuffed animal

237
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
or any object that evokes a feeling of home.

238
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Explain that these are symbols

239
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
that his family is safe and well

240
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and they're excited for him to be at camp

241
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and they look forward to hearing from them

242
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,000
when camp is over.

243
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Parents should discuss any plan

244
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
for contact with their scouts

245
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and they should both agree

246
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
to abide by that plan.

247
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
And, of course, you want to discuss

248
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
how camp will be a great opportunity

249
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
to strengthen their growing independence

250
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
and their ability to look after themselves.

251
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
It's perfectly understandable

252
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
that parents are going to be anxious

253
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
about any extended separation from their scout.

254
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
That anxiety sometimes makes the scout anxious.

255
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Sometimes parents are anxious

256
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
that the scout will be homesick

257
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and that kind of creates a feedback loop

258
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
that will ultimately make the scout homesick.

259
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
So if you sit down and discuss with your son

260
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
that homesickness is a pretty natural reaction

261
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
that some people have to being away

262
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
from familiar surroundings

263
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and they shouldn't be frightened by it.

264
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
you can talk about different ways to manage it

265
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and you can express the confidence

266
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
that your scout is going to be able to cope,

267
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
should they feel homesick,

268
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and help him with these

269
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
specific coping strategies.

270
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
If you feel homesick,

271
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
you need to talk to your friends

272
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and you need to talk to your scout leaders.

273
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Don't feel like you're alone

274
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
in feeling the way that you do

275
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
or you're ashamed of saying how that you feel.

276
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
You want to keep busy and have fun

277
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
and participate in the camp program,

278
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
even if you don't feel like it at first,

279
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and you can always write a letter

280
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
or a card home to us

281
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
and let us know how you're doing

282
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
Now. those are good coping strategies for scouts,

283
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
but there are also ways

284
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
that parents can complicate the issue

285
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
and they can make homesickness more likely.

286
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
If they indicate their own anxiety

287
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
with the pending separation.

288
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
by saying things like

289
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
we don't know what we'll do without you,

290
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
they can unwittingly undermine

291
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
their scout's ability to cope with the situation.

292
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
By no means should parents

293
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
discuss any kind of a rescue plan

294
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Like if you don't like it,

295
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
we'll come and pick you up

296
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
or you can always call us

297
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and we'll come and get you.

298
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
that sets a negative expectation

299
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
that's difficult for a scout to overcome.

300
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
you're basically telling them: well,

301
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
we know you're not going to like camp,

302
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
so if you get there- and that's true-

303
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
that kind of a rescue plan

304
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
undermines the experience for your scout

305
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
and it's pretty good guarantee

306
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
that you're going to get a call

307
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and you're going to have a homesick scout.

308
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Now, in the cell phone age,

309
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
parents and scout leaders

310
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
need to think through a parent's response

311
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
should a scout manage to contact home.

312
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
Let's remember that

313
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
real-time contact

314
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
is not a good thing

315
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
for alleviating homesickness.

316
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
they tend to make it worse,

317
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and this information comes to me

318
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
from both from my own experience

319
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
and from several studies

320
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
that I've looked at

321
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
dealing with students and with campers

322
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
who are away from home

323
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
for different periods of time.

324
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
it turns out that up to several weeks away from home,

325
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
these real-time contacts

326
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
aggravate homesickness.

327
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
if the stay away from home

328
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
is going to be more than several weeks,

329
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
real-time contacts help

330
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
alleviate homesickness.

331
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
so what happens if a scout

332
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
somehow gets to a phone

333
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
and calls home?

334
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
a parent should be prepared to say something like

335
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
we agreed before you left on how we'd be in contact.

336
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
if this is an emergency,

337
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
you need to go to a scout leader

338
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
or another responsible adult there at camp

339
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
and I will contact the camp later today

340
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
to check in and make sure that

341
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
everything's okay.

342
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
but right now I'm going to have to go

343
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
because we agreed that we wouldn't be doing this.

344
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
it's almost always a mistake

345
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
for a parent to insist on talking

346
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
to a homesick scout

347
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
or that a scout have a cell phone at camp.

348
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
this often accentuates the anxiety

349
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
for everybody involved-

350
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
for the parents and for the scouts

351
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
and the scout leaders involved.

352
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
if a parent insists on checking in,

353
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
they should discuss precisely

354
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
how this would happen

355
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
and talk only to the adult leaders

356
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
who can give them a progress report

357
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
on how the boy is doing.

358
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
so what happens if we take all these

359
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
measures and we have the contact plan

360
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
and we've done all these things and a scout

361
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
still gets homesick? well,

362
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
that's a pretty natural thing, that's going to happen

363
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
and you should expect it to happen.

364
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
should a scout come to you

365
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
and tell you that they're homesick,

366
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
you need to respond with a great deal of patience

367
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and caring and kindness.

368
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
being dismissive or abrupt with homesick

369
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
scouts is not going to help them

370
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
listen to what they have them say

371
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
and help them cope

372
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
again by staying busy

373
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
having fun or writing a letter home

374
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
or talking to their friends.

375
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
chiefly, it's important that we send the message

376
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
that there's no shame in being homesick.

377
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
everybody feels that way

378
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
at some point in their lives,

379
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
but we get over it and we get better.

380
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
homesickness is common.

381
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
good prevention can make it less

382
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
powerful. by familiarizing

383
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
scouts with the camp experience

384
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
and building transitions between home

385
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and camp and establishing a contact

386
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
plan, you'll prevent the most

387
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
common causes of homesickness in scouts.

388
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
write me a letter,

389
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
send it by name

390
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
email, that is folks.

391
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and here's an answer to one of your

392
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
emails.

393
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
we received this email from

394
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Jeff Zook,

395
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
who is with troop 228 in Oxnard, California.

396
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
he wrote in and said:

397
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
I'm not really sure if this is a problem.

398
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
I have many adults attending our outings,

399
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
sometimes equal to the number of scouts

400
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
attending. I have been very diligent

401
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
making sure the adults stay in the

402
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
adult camp and put their hands in their pockets.

403
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
most of them do. I do have

404
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
a couple of dads that step in

405
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
because their sons, who are patrol

406
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
leaders, bring a problem to their attention

407
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and the dads swoop in to help out.

408
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I'm working on this,

409
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
but any suggestion would be helpful.

410
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
many of the other adults simply enjoy the outdoors

411
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
and getting away from it all

412
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
one weekend a month.

413
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
do you see this as a problem now or in the future?

414
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
well, Jeff, thanks so much for getting in touch.

415
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
what you're talking about can be a bit of a problem.

416
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
it's obviously creating

417
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
a bit of a problem for you now.

418
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
you have a couple of dads who are very concerned about

419
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
making sure that things go smoothly.

420
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
that's to be expected.

421
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
it's a very natural reaction, but it's not always

422
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
the best thing for scouts.

423
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
with that many adults getting used to

424
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
scouting in hand for scouting events,

425
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
it can become a problem

426
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
in the future. I think that

427
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
you may sit down with your troop committee

428
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and establish some

429
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
rules for adults who are going to go camping

430
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
now. we know

431
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
that adult volunteers are a key element

432
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
of scouting, but they also have

433
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
a distinct role that

434
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
differentiates them from youth leaders.

435
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
in my experience,

436
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
scouts need the independence to run their own

437
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
program and solve their own problems,

438
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
and, this in mind,

439
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
here's what I suggest

440
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
the committee and you should get together

441
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
and draft as an understanding.

442
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
number one is that registered

443
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
volunteers have a standing invitation

444
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
to attend camping trips and meetings

445
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and events.

446
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
attendance of adults may be limited

447
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
to preserve a workable ratio of scouts

448
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
to adults.

449
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
a maximum of one adult to three scouts

450
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
should be kind of like your rule of thumb

451
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
and just as an aside

452
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
policy statement, that is not

453
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
something that I have derived from

454
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
scouting literature. I'm saying that

455
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
one to three

456
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
maximum. in my experience

457
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
that seems to work pretty good,

458
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
not to say that one to three

459
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
is a minimum in any sense

460
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
of the word. number two: parents

461
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
who are not registered as volunteers

462
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
may attend only camping trips

463
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
and events by invitation of the patrol

464
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
leaders council or the scout master

465
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
if sufficient registered

466
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
volunteers are unavailable to support the trip.

467
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
naturally,

468
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
any activity you have in scouting

469
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
is open to observation by parents

470
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
and arrangements can be made to attend

471
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
if there's a specific concern

472
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
from a specific parent or something like

473
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
that. but the idea, I think,

474
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
of an open invitation to all parents

475
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
for every camping trip,

476
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
anytime they want to go, is going to tip

477
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
that ratio of adults

478
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
to scouts. and as you said, Jeff,

479
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
you're having some trips. you have an adult to scout

480
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
ratio of one to one, having the understanding

481
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
that parents are welcome, they can

482
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
observe any time if they need to.

483
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
but for camping trips

484
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
it's going to be by invitation of the patrol

485
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
leaders council and then you may encourage

486
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
the patrol leaders council to establish

487
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
one weekend, that is, a family camp

488
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
or, you know, a parent

489
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
and son kind of camping experience.

490
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
and then, finally, what I would have

491
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
in your little memorandum of

492
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
understanding that comes from the committee

493
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
is that all adults- volunteers

494
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
and parents are too cheerfully

495
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and obediently follow the directions

496
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
of the scout master. now, while

497
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
we're on a scout camping trip, it's

498
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
understood that adults should be

499
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
camping in a specific section of the

500
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
campsite in their own tents,

501
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
so no adult shares a tent with their son,

502
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
and that adults will agree

503
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
to remain in the adult area throughout

504
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
their stay or to at least

505
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
remain in respectful distance from the

506
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
scout and the area and the distance

507
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
is determined by the scout master.

508
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
so other than the scout master

509
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
or those adults that he designates

510
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
will be directing

511
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
or instructing or advising or otherwise

512
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
really communicating

513
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
with the scouts. that's kind of

514
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
a quick take on what I would have

515
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
as an understanding. it gives the scout master

516
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
pretty much carte blanche to direct

517
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
and define what goes on without having

518
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
to explain themselves constantly.

519
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
I think you're right that adults do enjoy

520
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
camping once a month and getting away from it all.

521
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
that's great, but

522
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
what's going on a weekend camping trip has

523
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
nothing to do even remotely

524
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
with adults getting to

525
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
go out and have a relaxing weekend,

526
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
getting away from it all in the woods.

527
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
it's about the scouts and it's only

528
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
about the scouts. adults are

529
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
there, so scouts can go camping.

530
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
that's it. my favorite way to

531
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
